<article class="env-remark" id="068K">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/068K">Remark <span data-tag="068K">9.2.7.11</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a Kan complex. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The Kan complex $X$ is finitely dominated in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="067W" href="/tag/067W">9.2.6.6</a>: that is, it is a retract of an essentially finite Kan complex $Y$ (in the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{Kan}}$). 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The Kan complex $X$ is finitely dominated when regarded as an $\infty $-category, in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="068J" href="/tag/068J">9.2.7.10</a>: that is, it is a retract of an essentially finite $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (in the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{QCat}}$). 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> The implication $(1) \Rightarrow (2)$ is immediate. For the converse, assume that $X$ is a retract of an essentially finite $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Then $X$ is a retract of the Kan complex $\operatorname{Ex}^{\infty }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$, which is also essentially finite (Remark <a data-tag="068A" href="/tag/068A">9.2.7.5</a>). </p>
</article>
